iticism  that it had inadequately funded aids  research  this is a top priority with us by then  more than 3600 americans  had died from aids that was a very  dark time in our country paul olsen is  chief communications officer for gmhc  an organization founded in 1982 as  gay men's health crisis the first hiv  aids service organization in the world  at that time our government was not  stepping up  and assuming their responsibility for  responding to this crisis because  it was primarily affecting a community  that was  deeply marginalized at the time sensing  a lack of help from the government aids  activists  including a group that disrupted the cbs  evening news in one  instance the story at home is aids and  the television is responsible to cover  that  pushed for change  along with activism cultural flash  points put aids into the public  consciousness  the death of hollywood actor rock hudson  a gloveless princess diana shaking hands  with aids patients  in the uk and magic johnson's  shocking retirement announcement in  which he revealed he was hiv positive  hiv virus that i have attained  i will have to retire from the lakers  and in the mid 90s a medical  breakthrough  the development of anti-retroviral  treatment  that turned hiv aids from a virtual  death sentence  to a manageable disease today more than  two-thirds of those living with hiv  are receiving that treatment but the  epidemic remains in what paul olsen  calls the last mile what are some  misconceptions paul that still exists  today  about hiv aids i think the biggest  misconception is that the epidemic is  over  and we still  are seeing the unacceptably high numbers  of new infections especially in  black and brown and low-income  communities that are  disproportionately affected by other  infectious diseases  where do things stand now since you  first submitted that article to the cdc  there's been a remarkable developments  in both treatment and prevention over  these last 40 years  but today a person aged 25 diagnosed hiv  positive and who's able to  get on medication right away is  projected to have a virtually normal  lifespan  and that is a big big change from what  it was  in 1981. did you think  that the medical community would get to  that point where you would be able  to say that to a patient 40 years after  you submitted that paper  no i didn't the goal of the united  nations aids task force  is to end the epidemic by 2030. they  have what is called the 90 90 90  target they want 90 percent of those  infected with hiv  to know they are infected 90 percent  receiving  antiretroviral treatment and 90  of those who are receiving treatment to  have viral suppression to where they can  no longer  transmit the virus now it's ambitious  but the numbers today  are not far off from those targets but  just listening uh to the doctor there  one of the things that is so  credited with helping to change public  attitudes on this  are those public figures coming out and  doing what they did  and speaking about it in a way that at  the time was considered taboo so  that really does go a long way to  changing people's  mindsets but it changes it changed some  of the prejudice because  those were people that the public knew  that's right 30 years ago  who would have imagined johnson so many  people  like him still alive right at the you  know a top form  it just um it makes you believe  in something greater yeah it just does  progress has been made they didn't even  have a name for it 40 years ago still no  vaccine but the the treatment options  have been just  remarkable a lot of work still to do  this gotta have money though  for some of those treatment options
 

